        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Why does my treadmill click when the incline changes?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A click only during incline changes points to the incline motor, its gear or the lift arms. Check nothing is trapped under the deck, then run the incline calibration your manual describes. If the click continues or the incline sticks, contact support.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why is my treadmill making a clicking noise?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually a belt seam or debris (once per belt revolution), a loose bolt or the deck (with each step), or the drive belt, pulley or a roller bearing (with nobody on, speeding up with the belt). Time the click to find which.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why does my treadmill click when I walk?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A click that matches your footsteps is usually a loose frame or upright bolt, the deck moving on its mounts, an uneven floor or a dry deck. Tighten the bolts with the treadmill unplugged, level it and lubricate as the manual says.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a clicking treadmill belt normal?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A faint click from the belt seam passing over a roller is common and harmless. A seam that is lifting or fraying, or a click that gets louder, needs attention.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why is my treadmill motor clicking?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              With the belt running empty, a click from the front points to the drive belt, motor pulley, something touching the flywheel, or worn brushes on a brushed DC motor. Unplug before opening the motor cover, and use support if the machine is under warranty.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I keep using a clicking treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              If the click is the only symptom, yes, while you find it. Stop if it comes with a burning smell, a hesitating or surging belt, grinding, tripping or a visible crack.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I stop my treadmill from clicking?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Clean under the belt, lubricate the deck as the manual says, tighten the frame and upright bolts, re-centre the belt and level the machine. If it persists with nobody on, check the drive belt and pulley.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

